76501723.answer:
The 13 documents are expected, not a sign of a broken file. With no JSON pointer, JSONLoader loads every string it finds in the JSON object, so each string value becomes its own Document, not each record [1]. The tests confirm this: a nested JSON file produces one Document per string, with a `line` number in the metadata that counts up per string [3]. Your three records hold 13 strings, so you get 13 Documents, each with a `line` counter [1][3].

To extract only certain keys, pass a JSON pointer array as the second argument. This yields one Document per matching value [1]:

```typescript
import { JSONLoader } from "langchain/document_loaders/fs/json";

const loader = new JSONLoader(
  "src/document_loaders/example_data/example.json",
  ["/from", "/surname"]
);

const docs = await loader.load();
```

The documents do not show how to keep each whole record as one Document in the JS loader, so I can't say how to get exactly 3 Documents. Separately, the sample JSON in your question is missing commas after the `title` values. The documents do not cover that.

For reference, the Python JSONLoader works differently: it uses a `jq_schema`, and with `text_content=False` it can turn each object into a page content string [5][7]. That is Python, not langchain.js.

